The report is finished: `/Users/hosanglee/archy/reports/LLM code and software architecture.md`.

Its main conclusion is that archy should add checks that run after an edit, not more context handed to the agent before it. Outside studies (ETH Zurich on AGENTS.md files, SlopCodeBench, the CodeCompass code-graph study) now reproduce archy's six null results on giving the agent structure up front. Checks after the edit are the one placement with positive results.

Round 1 of the loop is running now. Four reviewers are each trying to disprove two of the report's ranked candidates:
- the after-edit hook and violation messages that name the allowed import route;
- a duplicates check limited to functions a PR adds, and a review summary posted on the PR;
- a weekly drift check and a draft rule file generated from the current imports;
- the before-versus-after experiment, the new-competitor positioning, and the parallel-agent overlap check.

The search for new `good first issue` tickets is also still running.

